1. `parser`, dates read month first: [#1](https://github.com/kulfix/mega-what-it-shows/issues/1)
2. `hint`, booking-v3 asks for the month first: [#2](https://github.com/kulfix/mega-what-it-shows/issues/2)

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued fix is the parser fix. It closes #1 only. Closing #4471 with it leaves #2 without a fix.
</Callout>

## What the desk's view shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The counts are the desk's own, from the export: each id once, placed by tenant, not by intake's tag.
Each ticket carries the count of its row here and lists the ids it covers.

- **`parser`** is #1: this whole row, the one-day moves under the table included. The desk files those
  under `parser` by tenant and footnotes them; #1 does the same.
- **`hint`** is #2: this whole row. The view still says it has no ticket of its own; it has one now.

## The problems

### `parser`: every date is read month first

The shared parser reads dates month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so any date whose
day is 12 or less comes out with day and month swapped: a booking for 7 April lands on 4 July. This is
what #4471 describes, and its queued fix is the fix for this problem. Most of these reports show the swap
in their own dates; a few only say the day was wrong, and land here by their tenant and channel.

### `hint`: booking-v3 asks for the month first

The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, for tenants who write the day first.
People type what the hint asks; the form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The result looks the same
as `parser`, but the parser fix does not touch it: the hint has to follow the tenant's locale. Only
reports sent through the form of a booking-v3 tenant land here.

### The footnote: reports read as a one-day move

> Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting. We held it twice.

The desk read a few `parser` reports as a date moved by one day, or an invite that arrived before its
booking was made. They stay in #1, where the desk files them. A month/day swap does not explain a
one-day move, so re-check them when the parser fix ships; if they still happen, they need their own
reproduction.

## Lessons

- A subject line says how a report feels, not what broke. Different bugs share "wrong day".
- Before calling a merged ticket known, read what its own table shows: here, a row marked "no ticket of
  its own" and a footnote.
- Count with the desk's own view: one id once, by tenant, footnotes where they're filed.

